# Response headers for the Cloudflare Workers static-assets deployment.
#
# Copied verbatim from public/ into dist/ at build time, which is the directory
# wrangler.jsonc points `assets` at. Cloudflare reads it from there; it is never
# served as a file itself.
#
# Every directive below was checked against the BUILT output rather than against
# the source, per the house rule. The counts quoted are from `dist/` and are the
# reason each directive is shaped the way it is.
#
# What this file CANNOT do: redirect http:// to https://. That happens at the
# edge, before any header rule is consulted, and is a zone setting
# (SSL/TLS -> Edge Certificates -> Always Use HTTPS). Strict-Transport-Security
# is deliberately absent here for the same reason: Cloudflare's HSTS control
# emits it, and setting it in two places is how the two drift apart.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'sha256-IpTuWWqwbljHiMYBxlWBc1+AV6SnVVgqrCOAHhEYbRk=' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

# Notes on the policy above, directive by directive:
#
#   default-src 'none'   The site's own build ships one script, inline, and 0
#                        .js/.mjs/.map files. Every other <script> tag it emits
#                        is a type="application/ld+json" data block, which the
#                        parser never prepares as script and script-src
#                        therefore never reaches. check:built asserts all of
#                        it. So the default is the strictest value that exists,
#                        and anything not listed below is refused outright.
#
#   script-src 'sha256'  The one script of ours: ZoomMap's mouse controls on
#                        /datacentres/ (wheel zoom, drag to pan), added on the
#                        owner's instruction. Allowed by the hash of its exact
#                        text, not by 'self' or 'unsafe-inline', so it is the
#                        only inline code that can run and no file from this
#                        origin can. Any edit to the script changes the hash;
#                        check:built recomputes it from dist, prints the new
#                        value, and fails until this line carries it. It also
#                        fails on a hash here that no script uses any more.
#
#   script-src host      Exactly one host, and it is not ours. Cloudflare Web
#                        Analytics is enabled on the zone, and Cloudflare injects
#                        its beacon into the HTML at the edge, AFTER the build.
#                        Nothing in this repository puts it there, which is
#                        precisely why it went unnoticed for so long: it is
#                        injected only for browser-like requests, so fetching the
#                        page with curl shows a clean document.
#
#                        Naming the host here is the point. A bare 'self' would
#                        not have allowed it, and the first version of this file
#                        did not name it at all, which blocked the analytics the
#                        site owner actually wants. Listing it means the exception
#                        is visible, deliberate, and the ONLY script origin that
#                        will ever run. Anything else - an injected script from a
#                        compromised feed included - is still inert.
#
#                        The beacon carries its own integrity hash, so the file
#                        is verified by the browser before it runs.
#
#   connect-src          Where the beacon sends its measurements, and the first
#                        version of this line was wrong in a way worth recording.
#
#                        It listed only https://cloudflareinsights.com, on the
#                        reasonable assumption that a Cloudflare beacon reports
#                        to Cloudflare. It does not. It POSTs to /cdn-cgi/rum on
#                        THIS domain, which Cloudflare intercepts at the edge, so
#                        the request is same-origin and 'self' is what permits it.
#                        The external host stays listed because some
#                        configurations do post there directly.
#
#                        The failure mode is the reason to be careful here: the
#                        script loaded fine and the page looked perfect, and the
#                        only sign anything was wrong was one line in the browser
#                        console. Analytics that collect nothing while appearing
#                        to work is worse than analytics that are plainly off.
#                        Found by opening the deployed site in a real browser,
#                        which is the only place it was visible.
#
#   style-src            'unsafe-inline' is required today and is not cosmetic:
#                        dist carries 1451 inline style= attributes (every SVG
#                        map shape carries its own fill) and 30 inline <style>
#                        blocks (Astro's scoped styles, plus the per-period
#                        rules TimeMap generates). Removing it would unstyle the
#                        maps entirely. Astro 5.18 ships experimental.csp, which
#                        would hash these and let 'unsafe-inline' go; that is a
#                        separate change, not a security fix, and it is not made
#                        here.
#
#   font-src 'self'      11 woff2 files, all served from /_astro/. No font
#                        network is contacted, and this keeps it that way.
#
#   img-src 'self'       There are 0 <img> tags. This covers the icon links
#                        (140 link elements, four per page, resolving to three
#                        URLs: /favicon.svg is named twice, by rel=icon and by
#                        rel=mask-icon, plus /favicon.ico and
#                        /apple-touch-icon.png) and the one og:image, which is
#                        fetched by other platforms rather than by a reader's
#                        browser. It blocks an injected tracking pixel.
#
#   base-uri 'none'      The site uses no <base>. An injected one would silently
#                        repoint all 1777 internal links, which is the number
#                        check-internal-links.mjs prints when it passes.
#
#   form-action 'none'   There are no forms anywhere. Nothing should be able to
#                        post from this origin.
#
#   frame-ancestors      The site has no sensitive action to trick anyone into,
#                        but it also has no reason to be framed, and being
#                        framed inside a page that misattributes the data is a
#                        real way to damage a citation index. X-Frame-Options is
#                        deliberately not also sent: frame-ancestors supersedes
#                        it in every browser that matters.
#
#   nosniff              Stops a browser deciding that .txt or .xml is really
#                        something executable.
#
#   Referrer-Policy      The site links out 1311 times. Without this, the full
#                        path of a jurisdiction or news-category page is handed
#                        to every government publisher we cite. Origin-only is
#                        enough for them and tells them less about the reader.
#                        Not no-referrer, because some government hosts behave
#                        oddly when it is absent entirely.
#
#   Permissions-Policy   Denies features nothing here uses. interest-cohort is
#                        not listed: it was never standardised and now only
#                        produces console noise.
#
# Every count above was taken from the current dist by the same definition it
# states, and the script ones are asserted rather than trusted: check:built fails
# if any page carries a script with a src, or an inline script whose hash is not
# in script-src, or if script-src lists a hash nothing uses, or if dist holds a
# .js, .mjs or .map file. The rest move with the site, so treat a mismatch as a signal to recount
# rather than as a reason to widen a directive.

# Content-hashed build assets. The hash IS the version, so a changed file gets a
# changed URL and these can be cached for a year. Applied only to /_astro/*, and
# deliberately NOT to HTML, robots.txt or the sitemaps, which keep the
# revalidate-every-time default so a data refresh is visible immediately.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# /404.html is served at the literal path /404 with a 200, because Cloudflare
# treats it as an ordinary asset when that exact path is requested; only an
# UNMATCHED path gets it with a 404 status, via assets.not_found_handling.
# So the URL itself is indexable and says "there is no page at that address",
# which is a soft 404. The wildcard covers /404, /404.html and /404/, all three
# of which serve that file, and matches no other route on the site; a genuine
# 404 at some other path is untouched and keeps the status code it already
# returns. Deliberately a header and not a <meta name="robots">: check:built
# asserts that NO page carries a noindex meta, and that assertion guards 34
# pages.
/404*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
